# LUCI Case Studies

**When to use this:** whenever you're creating or editing a **LUCI case study** (customer success story) — wherever the work starts, even before the file lands in `ui_kits/case-studies/`. If the piece is a case study, this rule applies; the header/title treatment below is the go-to.

**Scope — case studies only.** Do **not** apply this header/title treatment, the split-title pattern, or any rule here to newsletters, sales documents, the website, the hub, emails, or any other asset. Those assets have their own rules (`luci-visual-design.mdc`, `luci-modern-design-guidelines.mdc`, `luci-sales-document-system.mdc`, `luci-website-review-deploy.mdc`, etc.). This rule governs **case study pages only**; everything here is case-study-scoped.

Canonical pattern for **case study pages** — web-first (published on lucisystems.com via Webflow) with a print/PDF variant. Inherits Track A (web) / Track C (print) foundations from `luci-modern-design-guidelines.mdc` and brand/voice from `luci-messaging-voice.mdc` / `luci-visual-design.mdc`.

This rule canonizes **three things**: (1) the **narrative spine** — consolidation and single-interface as the argument of the page, not footer boilerplate; (2) the **hero header / title treatment** and document structure; (3) the **visual template** — section backgrounds, photo treatments, and color decisions that give every case study a consistent look.

**Reference implementations:**
- **Visual template (canonical):** `ui_kits/case-studies/sams-town.html` + `luci-website/src/components/CaseStudySamsTown.astro` + `luci-website/src/data/caseStudySamsTown.ts` — the standing visual template: section backgrounds, photo-band, inline photo, scope story, color treatments. New case studies start here and adapt.
- **Copy spine (target):** `ui_kits/case-studies/tachi-palace.html` — scope inventory, “what runs now,” subtraction, operator-facing results.
- **Layout / variants:** `ui_kits/case-studies/ameristar-council-bluffs.html` + `-webflow-embed.html` / `-webflow-body.html` (Webflow) + `-print.html` (PDF). Wrapper: `.luci-cs`.

