## Narrative spine — consolidation first (CANONICAL)

Every LUCI case study is a **consolidation story told with operational proof**. The reader should know *what LUCI runs*, *what went away*, and *how staff use one interface* before they finish the property context.

**Core thesis (repeatable):** fragmented A/V, signage, and control silos → **one interface** the property team runs from anywhere → fewer systems, racks, and workflows to manage.

**LUCI already leads with subtraction in brand voice** (`luci-messaging-voice.mdc`, Messaging Guide worldview). Case studies must make subtraction **verifiable** — named systems decommissioned, licenses lapsed, vendors gone — not abstract “fewer variables.”

**Register:** operational and institutional. What *reads* revenue-forward in competitor assets is usually **scope rendered concretely** (displays, zones, square footage under management) — not ROI, handle lift, or payback. Use **scope stats**, not outcome stats that require quarters of operator data. Case studies should be **shippable the day handoff completes**.

**“Single interface” placement:** hero dek, glance summary, or a named mid-page section — **never only** in the footer “About LUCI” boilerplate. If the CTA repeats the value prop, the reader should already know the thesis.

### Denominator first — many → one (CANONICAL)

Build the **denominator before the benefit**. Stack scope stats (endpoints, zones, walls, speakers, machines on network) **before** claiming one interface, one login, or less training.

- **Benefit-first** (“one unified view,” “less training”) reads as a claim to trust.
- **Denominator-first** (70+ displays, 180+ speakers, eight walls → one interface) lets the reader reach the same conclusion.

The story is arithmetic: **many → one**. Do not open results or the one-interface section with abstract benefits until the stat block has established volume.

### Scope stats, not outcome stats

**Use:** counts of what LUCI runs or replaced — displays, tickers, audio zones, LED walls, install window, racks removed, simultaneous sources.

**Do not wait for or invent:** revenue lift, F&B impact, handle %, payback period, guest satisfaction scores. Those block publishing and violate voice rules on percentage claims.

**Do not lead with borrowed project capex** ($144M redevelopment, nine-figure new-build totals) as authority — impressive but not LUCI’s number; it scopes the product as mega-build-only and makes a $2M refresh reader self-disqualify. One short venue line for context; **scope-under-management stats** carry scale.

### Page spine (section order)

Follow this order unless the project genuinely lacks a beat (say so in draft notes — do not skip inventory):

| # | Beat | Job |
|---|------|-----|
| 1 | **Hero** | Split title (speed/transition may live in lead-in + Syncopate phrase). **Dek names consolidation** — what was unified, not just project duration. |
| 2 | **At a glance** | Challenge / approach / result row **plus** **scope stat block first** — 4–6 counts of surface area under management (denominator). |
| 3 | **Challenge** | Short. Silos + **volume** (how many systems/zones/endpoints were fragmented) — not only “aging equipment.” |
| 4 | **Approach** | Embedded execution (install window, data-center move, on-site team, training/handoff) **and** what got consolidated. |
| 5 | **One interface** | **One claim in the H2** — e.g. “One interface. Every endpoint.” Not three parallel claims (scale + simplicity + design). **Concrete scene** here or next: one moment, one person, one screen (fight night, bingo call, floor reset — property-specific). Then plain workflow language. |
| 6 | **Consolidation you can see** | **Canonical section** — fixed H2 verbatim (homepage `homeLedger.kicker`). 4–6 social-ready removal/consolidation rows. See markup pattern below. |
| 7 | **Results** | Operational outcomes; **tie bullets to numbers or named capabilities** where possible (“eight LED walls,” “every audio zone,” not “unified view” alone). |
| 8 | **Forward** | Property transition / expansion (boat → land, one room → whole resort). **LUCI-only** — amplify this; competitors rarely own it. |
| 9 | **CTA** | Short “About LUCI”; optional verbatim boilerplate — reader already has the spine. |

### Hero dek — thesis before timeline

The Syncopate phrase may celebrate **speed or readiness** (“one week,” “future-ready property”). The **dek must still answer**: what fragmented stack became one interface?

**Pass:** “LUCI unified [scope] on one interface — [install window / transition hook].”  
**Fail:** dek is only duration + “modernized AV” with no consolidation or scope.

### At a glance — scope inventory / denominator (required)

Add a **facts row or stat grid** immediately after the hero — **before** narrative prose argues one interface.

Pull real project numbers; approximate only when exact counts are unavailable — label honestly in draft notes.

**Inventory examples (scope under management):** displays · LED walls / tickers · audio zones · speakers · gaming or floor endpoints on network · sportsbook layouts · install duration · racks or head-ends removed · simultaneous sources.

This block is the **denominator**. Abstract “unified control” without counts fails the format.

### One interface section — concrete scene + operator moment

**Concrete scene (required):** One property-specific moment — time, place, one operator, one screen, one action sequence. Beats isolated stats when written tightly. Place this **in or directly under** the one-interface H2 — do not bury the best operational detail in a late section.

**Workflow:** Plain language for what staff used to do vs. one action now (search/select/send, zone volume, preset fire, channel swap, scheduled reset).

**Quotes — credibility order:**
1. **Property operator** (director of ops, AV lead, facilities, slot/bingo manager) — **required when available**; one operator quote beats any integrator quote for trust.
2. **LUCI FDE / embedded team** — proves hands-on delivery.
3. **Integrator or vendor** — optional; never the only voice in the asset. If every quote is from someone with a commercial stake in the sale, the piece fails credibility vs. competitors who make the same mistake.

### Subtraction — verifiable removals (brand voice made provable)

Per `luci-messaging-voice.mdc`, LUCI leads with what gets **removed**. In case studies, subtraction must **name line items** — verifiable, more credible than capability adds:

- Standalone signage CMS or control platform **decommissioned** (name it when client approves)
- External media players or redundant head-ends **removed**
- Vendor or license **no longer in the building**
- Parallel workflow or manual job **eliminated**
- Infrastructure **not built** because processing moved (e.g. data center vs. new AV rooms)

**Standing intake — ask on every install close** (for case study draft + handoff notes):
- What was decommissioned?
- Which license lapsed or contract ended?
- Which vendor stopped being on property?
- What task does nobody do anymore?
- What rack, room, or interface disappeared?

Capture answers in project notes even if the case study ships later.

### Consolidation you can see — canonical section (REQUIRED)

Every case study includes this section — **same title every time**, matching the homepage consolidation ledger kicker (`homeLedger.kicker` in `luci-website/src/data/site.ts`):

**Section H2 (verbatim, do not paraphrase):** `Consolidation you can see`

Place after **Approach**, before **Quote** or **Results**. Light canvas (`sec--light` / `cs-sec--light`).

**Markup pattern:**

```html
<section class="sec sec--light cs-consolidation" aria-labelledby="cs-consolidation-title">
  <header class="cs-consolidation__header">
    <span class="cs-consolidation__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <h2 id="cs-consolidation-title" class="cs-consolidation__title">Consolidation you can see</h2>
  </header>
  <ul class="cs-consolidation__list">
    <li class="cs-consolidation__item">
      <p class="cs-consolidation__item-title">Short removal headline</p>
      <p class="cs-consolidation__item-text">One sentence — what left, merged, or stopped.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>
```

(Tachi uses `cs-sec cs-sec--light`; Ameristar uses `sec sec--light` — same `.cs-consolidation` block classes.)

**Row rules:**
- **4–6 items** per property, from install-close intake.
- Each row = **one verifiable consolidation/removal** — title names the thing; body states what happened.
- **Social-ready:** each `.cs-consolidation__item` is a standalone card candidate (title → headline, text → body). Do not write rows that only make sense in paragraph context.
- No checkmarks or numbered grid — **hairline-separated stack** (de-boxed list).

**CSS:** mint 40×3px rule above title; title Space Grotesk 700; item titles bold; item text Inter or body stack per asset.

### LUCI-only beats — always look for these

Do not drop these for competitor-style guest glam:

- Install speed **with the floor still running**  
- Processing centralized (data center, land-side consolidation)  
- **Embedded team** on site — hang, wire, train, stay on  
- Multi-phase property transitions  
- Platform grows without per-seat licensing / parallel systems (when true for the project)

### Borrow vs. skip (competitive pattern)

**Borrow:** consolidation stats above the fold · named “one interface” section · endpoint inventory · “what we eliminated” beat · operator micro-story.

**Skip:** long property redevelopment / historic-building arc as main character · borrowed mega-project capex as lead authority · revenue / outcome metrics · integrator-only quotes · product SKU catalog · triple-claim section headers (“scale + simplicity + design” in one H2).

### Case-study voice exceptions

Named **property and operator** are required in the case-study format (unlike anonymous public marketing). **Scope counts and install durations** are encouraged — they are proof, not hype. Do not invent metrics; use project facts or approved client sign-off.

### Copy pre-ship (narrative)

1. **Denominator before benefit** — scope stat block precedes one-interface claim.  
2. Consolidation thesis in hero dek or glance — not footer-only.  
3. Scope inventory present (4+ stats of surface under management).  
4. **Named subtraction** in **Consolidation you can see** — verifiable rows, not abstract “fewer variables” alone.  
5. **Consolidation you can see** section present — exact H2, 4+ social-ready rows.  
6. **Concrete scene** — one operator moment in or under one-interface section.  
7. **Operator quote** when available — not integrator-only.  
8. Results tied to specifics, not abstraction alone.  
9. Forward-looking transition beat where the project has one.  
10. No borrowed capex lead · no outcome/revenue stats · voice: operational, declarative, **A/V** — no “layer” for LUCI, no retired hype terms.

