## Visual template — section rhythm and treatments (CANONICAL)

Sam's Town is the standing visual template. New case studies follow this rhythm and adapt content/media to what they have. The template is the **spine, not a straitjacket** — sections expand or retract based on content, and media choices flex (photo vs. video vs. collage, drag bar vs. side-by-side). What stays consistent: the narrative spine, the section order, the background rhythm, the color language, and the hero header.

### Section background rhythm

Alternate dark and light sections for contrast and pace. The default rhythm from Sam's Town:

| # | Beat | Background |
|---|------|-----------|
| 1 | Hero | `--navy-deep` (dark) + 4px mint bottom border |
| 2 | At a glance / Challenge | Light (`cs-sec--light`) |
| 3 | Challenge photo | **Photo-band** — full-bleed image, horizontal gradient scrim for text legibility, no bottom border (runs directly into next section) |
| 4 | Approach | Light |
| 5 | Racebook / One interface | Light with **inline photo** (single image, not a crew grid) |
| 6 | Wall they wanted / Mid | `cs-sec--mid` with **soft mint radial-gradient wash** (not circuit texture, not mesh) |
| 7 | Consolidation you can see | Light (`sec--light`) |
| 8 | Results | Light or dark — adapt to content |
| 9 | Scope story | Dark (`--navy-deep` or `--navy`) — stat grid with mint numbers, scope-story title (see below) |
| 10 | Forward / What's next | Light |
| 11 | CTA | Dark or light — adapt to content |

**Not every section needs its own background.** Merge short sections into adjacent ones; let the rhythm breathe. A section with no strong visual can stay light and let the copy carry it.

### Photo-band (full-bleed image with gradient)

For the "challenge" or "before" beat — the room they had, the old system, the problem:

- Full-bleed image spanning the entire section width.
- **Horizontal linear-gradient scrim** on the text side for legibility (dark side → transparent).
- **No bottom border** — the section runs directly into the next one.
- Text sits on the scrim side; image breathes on the other.

**CSS classes:** `.cs-photo-band`, `.cs-photo-band__img` (absolute, covers section), `.cs-photo-band__scrim` (gradient overlay), `.cs-photo-band__content` (text, z-index above scrim).

### Inline photo (single image)

For the "approach" or "bringing X on board" beat — a technician working, an install moment:

- Single image, not a multi-photo crew grid.
- Centered or side-aligned within the section, with breathing room.
- Caption optional; let the image speak when it's strong.

**CSS class:** `.cs-inline-photo` with `img` inside.

### Mid-section background — soft mint wash

For the "wall they wanted" or transitional mid-page beat:

- `cs-sec--mid` background uses a **radial-gradient mint wash** — soft, subtle, not a circuit texture or mesh pattern.
- The wash is positioned and sized to add atmosphere without competing with content.
- Mint is the primary accent; gold is secondary (per `luci-mint-primary-gold-secondary.mdc`).

**CSS:** `background: radial-gradient(circle at <position>, rgba(104,227,190,<opacity>) 0%, transparent <spread>);` on `--navy-deep` or light canvas — adapt to the section's base.

### Scope story title — color treatment

The "Property on LUCI to date" heading uses a two-color treatment:

- **"Property on LUCI"** (base text) → `--mint` (green carries the brand name)
- **"to date"** (the `<em>` accent) → `--off-white` (white is the temporal qualifier, quieter)

This is the inverse of the standard section-title pattern (white base + mint accent). The swap puts the brand emphasis on LUCI, not on the timeframe.

**CSS:** `.cs-scope-story__title { color: var(--mint); }` and `.cs-scope-story__title em { color: var(--off-white); }` (or `--accent-light` on light canvases).

### Flexibility — adapt the media to the content

The template specifies the **structure** and **visual language**, not the exact media. Adapt freely:

- **First image (challenge/before):** photo, video, or collage — whatever best shows the "before" state. Photo-band treatment is the default, but a standard inline image works if the photo doesn't need full-bleed.
- **Before/after (slider):** drag bar is the default, but **two photos side by side** is fine when the contrast is more important than the interactive reveal. Choose based on what makes the transformation clearest.
- **Install/approach image:** single photo, a short video loop, or a small grid — adapt to what you have. Don't force a grid when one strong photo tells the story.
- **Text length:** the narrative spine (denominator first, concrete scene, named subtraction) is the constraint, not word count. A section can be 3 sentences or 3 paragraphs — let the content dictate.

**Do not** drop a section entirely (say so in draft notes if the project lacks a beat), and **do not** add sections the spine doesn't call for. The spine is the contract; the media is flexible.

### Data structure (TypeScript)

New case studies on the website follow the `caseStudySamsTown.ts` data shape. Key fields:

- `property`, `location`, `operator` — hero property row
- `hero` — `leadin`, `titleAccent` (Syncopate phrase), `dek`, `photo`
- `challenge` — `titleBefore`, `titleAccent`, `body[]`, `photo` (photo-band)
- `approach` — `titleBefore`, `titleAccent`, `body[]`, `photo` (inline)
- `raceBook` / one-interface section — `titleBefore`, `titleAccent`, `body[]`, `photo` (inline)
- `consolidation` — `items[]` with `title` + `text` (canonical section)
- `results` — `titleBefore`, `titleAccent`, `body[]`
- `scopeStory` — `titleBefore` ("Property on LUCI"), `titleAccent` ("to date"), `stats[]`, `outcomeBefore`/`outcomeAccent`/`outcomeAfter`, `dek`
- `whatsNext` — `titleBefore`, `titleAccent`, `body[]`

Each case study gets its own `CaseStudy<Name>.astro` component and `caseStudy<Name>.ts` data file. The component handles the visual template; the data file holds the content. Swap content, keep the structure.

