## PDF — derivative, not primary

**Design web-first. PDF is a derivative, generated from the web version when a buyer asks for one.** Do not let print constraints limit the web design.

### Why web-first

- The web version is the primary deliverable — it's what lives on lucisystems.com and what the sales team links to.
- Web allows video, scroll interactions, full-bleed photos — things that make the case study *better* on screen.
- Designing for print first produces a flat document that happens to also be online; designing web-first produces a rich page that can be flattened to PDF when needed.

### Why links, not attachments

- **Trackable** — you can see if a prospect clicked, when, how often. A PDF attachment is a black box.
- **Always current** — no stale PDFs with old numbers floating around. The link always serves the latest version.
- **Less spam risk** — a personalized plain-text email with a single link to lucisystems.com is standard B2B practice and is *less* likely to hit spam filters than an email with a 5MB+ PDF attachment. Large attachments are one of the biggest spam triggers.
- **Mobile-friendly** — a link works on a phone; a PDF attachment is a download.

### When a buyer needs a PDF

Some procurement processes require a document. Generate one from the web version:

- `-print.html` variant on US Letter (see Ameristar's `-print.html` as the pattern).
- `@media print` CSS: page breaks, font embedding (use `assets/fonts/luci-brand-fonts.css`, no Google Fonts `<link>`), `print-color-adjust: exact` on dark sections so navy + mint render.
- Keep the same split title, same content, same spine — the PDF is the web page flattened, not a separate design.

This is a **30-minute task after the web version is done**, not a parallel design track.
